<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jim Crow laws</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1870s–<a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jim Crow laws were state and local laws enforcing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/racial-segregation">racial segregation</a> in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from the late nineteenth century until the 1960s. Upheld by the Supreme Court in Plessy v. Ferguson (1896), they segregated schools, transport and public places and went with measures that disenfranchised Black voters, until they were struck down by court rulings and the civil rights laws of 1964 and 1965.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jim-crow-laws.jpg" alt="Jim Crow laws" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Wilson · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_and_colored_water_fountain_then_and_now_-_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a> abolishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was ratified on 18 December 1865. During Reconstruction, between 1865 and 1877, federal laws protected the civil rights of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> in the South. In the 1870s Democrats gradually regained power in Southern legislatures while paramilitary groups such as the Ku Klux Klan, the White League and the Red Shirts drove out Republican officeholders and lynched Black voters to suppress the Black vote.</p><p>The term Jim Crow probably derives from Jump Jim Crow, a minstrel dance performed in blackface by the white actor Thomas D. Rice, first staged in 1828. By the end of the nineteenth century the name meant the social separation of the races.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The laws were passed by white-dominated state legislatures to remove the gains that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> had made during Reconstruction. Beginning in the 1870s they mandated segregation in public facilities across the South, and companion laws excluded most African Americans from the vote. The Civil Rights Act of 1875 guaranteed equal treatment in public accommodations but had little effect, and an 1883 Supreme Court decision found it unconstitutional in some respects.</p><p>In Plessy v. Ferguson in 1896 the Supreme Court upheld the doctrine of separate but equal facilities. In practice the facilities for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> were consistently inferior and underfunded, and sometimes did not exist. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">National Association for the Advancement of Colored People</a>, founded in 1909, campaigned against the laws and the doctrine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brown-v-board-of-education">Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka</a> in 1954 the Supreme Court unanimously held public school segregation unconstitutional and overturned Plessy. In 1955 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-parks">Rosa Parks</a> refused to give up her bus seat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgomery-alabama">Montgomery, Alabama</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgomery-bus-boycott">Montgomery bus boycott</a> that followed lasted more than a year. Mass demonstrations organised by the Southern Christian Leadership Conference, headed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a>, drew national attention on television.</p><p>Congress overcame Southern filibusters to pass the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964">Civil Rights Act of 1964</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a>, which overturned most remaining Jim Crow laws. Laws against interracial marriage were struck down in Loving v. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> in 1967. Historians count the laws among the causes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-migration-african-american">Great Migration</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> left the rural South for cities in the Northeast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midwestern-united-states">Midwest</a> and West.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jim Crow laws” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Confederacy and the Sons of Confederate Veterans in the decades after the war and again during the civil rights movement, built monuments and shaped textbooks to sustain white supremacist policies such as the <b>Jim Crow laws</b>; the battle flag's modern display began with the segregationist Dixiecrats in 1948.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Reconstruction amendments abolished slavery in 1865 and promised equal protection and the vote to Black men, but Southern states soon disenfranchised them and imposed segregation through <b>Jim Crow laws</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jim Crow laws</b> segregated schools, trains, streetcars, parks and hospitals, and in Plessy v. Ferguson (1896) the Supreme Court accepted “separate but equal” facilities.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>He first meant to write a poem for President Abraham Lincoln's birthday, but chose instead to write about the struggles of African Americans after the Reconstruction era, as <b>Jim Crow laws</b> were being passed across the South.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lift-every-voice-and-sing">Lift Every Voice and Sing</a></li><li><blockquote>The decision underpinned <b>Jim Crow laws</b> until Brown v. Board of Education in 1954.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-18">18 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
